{"id": "D", "title": "Chromosomal basis of inheritance", "lec": "Lecture 19", "camp": "Campbell Ch. 15",
 "qs": [
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "The chromosome theory of inheritance states that:",
   "o": ["genes are made of protein and are found in the cytoplasm",
         "Mendelian genes have specific loci on chromosomes, and it is the chromosomes that undergo segregation and independent assortment",
         "each chromosome carries only one gene", "chromosomes blend in the offspring"], "a": 1, "e": "The behaviour of chromosomes in meiosis explains Mendel’s laws.", "r": "15.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Morgan crossed a red-eyed (wild-type) female fruit fly with a white-eyed male. The F<sub>2</sub> showed 3 red : 1 white, but all white-eyed flies were male. He concluded that:",
   "o": ["the white-eye allele is dominant", "the eye-colour gene is on the Y chromosome",
         "the eye-colour gene is on the X chromosome, with no corresponding locus on the Y", "eye colour shows incomplete dominance"],
   "a": 2, "e": "F<sub>1</sub> were all red, so white is recessive; its restriction to males in F<sub>2</sub> shows it is X-linked.", "r": "15.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "In <i>Drosophila</i> notation, <i>w</i><sup>+</sup> represents:",
   "o": ["the mutant white-eye allele", "the wild-type (red-eye) allele", "a Y-linked allele", "a dominant lethal allele"], "a": 1,
   "e": "+ marks the wild type, the phenotype most common in natural populations.", "r": "15.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "For X-linked genes, a human male is described as:",
   "o": ["homozygous", "heterozygous", "hemizygous", "polyploid"], "a": 2, "e": "He has only one X, so only one allele of each X-linked gene.", "r": "15.2"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Which of these is an X-linked recessive disorder?",
   "o": ["Down syndrome", "Albinism", "Haemophilia", "Turner syndrome"], "a": 2,
   "e": "Others in the lecture: colour blindness (mostly X-linked) and Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Albinism is autosomal recessive; Down and Turner are chromosome-number disorders.", "r": "15.2"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "If homologous chromosomes fail to separate in meiosis I (nondisjunction), the four gametes will have:",
   "o": ["<i>n</i>+1, <i>n</i>+1, <i>n</i>−1, <i>n</i>−1", "<i>n</i>+1, <i>n</i>−1, <i>n</i>, <i>n</i>", "<i>n</i>, <i>n</i>, <i>n</i>, <i>n</i>", "2<i>n</i>, 2<i>n</i>, 0, 0"],
   "a": 0, "e": "All four are abnormal. Nondisjunction of sister chromatids in meiosis II gives <i>n</i>+1, <i>n</i>−1, <i>n</i>, <i>n</i>.", "r": "15.4"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "If sister chromatids fail to separate in one of the two cells in meiosis II, the gametes will have:",
   "o": ["<i>n</i>+1, <i>n</i>+1, <i>n</i>−1, <i>n</i>−1", "<i>n</i>+1, <i>n</i>−1, <i>n</i>, <i>n</i>", "all <i>n</i>", "all <i>n</i>+1"],
   "a": 1, "e": "Only the cell with the error makes abnormal gametes; the other meiosis II cell is normal.", "r": "15.4"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Which statement about Turner syndrome is correct?",
   "o": ["Karyotype XXY; male", "Trisomy 21", "Karyotype X0; female; the only known viable monosomy in humans", "Karyotype 3<i>n</i>"],
   "a": 2, "e": "Turner (X0) individuals are female and usually sterile. XXY = Klinefelter (male).", "r": "15.4"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "A zygote with one extra copy of a particular chromosome (2<i>n</i> + 1) is called:",
   "o": ["monosomic", "trisomic", "triploid", "tetraploid"], "a": 1, "e": "Monosomic = 2<i>n</i> − 1; triploid = 3<i>n</i> (a whole extra set).", "r": "15.4"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "The SRY gene is:",
   "o": ["on the X chromosome; it determines female development", "on the Y chromosome; its protein triggers development of male features (testes)",
         "on chromosome 21", "a gene for red–green colour vision"], "a": 1, "e": "SRY = Sex-determining Region of Y.", "r": "15.2"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Which statement about human sex chromosomes is correct?",
   "o": ["An ovum may carry an X or a Y chromosome", "The Y chromosome carries genes for many characters unrelated to sex",
         "Only the ends of the Y chromosome have regions homologous with the X", "Females are XY and males are XX"], "a": 2,
   "e": "Every ovum carries X; sperm are 50% X and 50% Y. The X carries many genes unrelated to sex; the Y mainly codes for sex determination.", "r": "15.2"},
  {"t": "match", "q": "Match the condition with its chromosome make-up.",
   "L": ["Down syndrome", "Klinefelter syndrome", "Turner syndrome", "Triploidy", "Monosomy"],
   "R": ["XXY (male)", "X0 (female)", "Trisomy 21", "2<i>n</i> − 1", "3<i>n</i>"], "a": [2, 0, 1, 4, 3],
   "e": "Down syndrome frequency rises with the mother’s age.", "r": "15.4"},
  {"t": "num", "q": "A colour-blind man (<i>X<sup>n</sup>Y</i>) marries a woman who is homozygous for normal vision (<i>X<sup>N</sup>X<sup>N</sup></i>). Find (a) P(a daughter is colour-blind), (b) the genotype of every daughter, (c) P(a son is colour-blind).",
   "a": "(a) 0 &nbsp; (b) all <i>X<sup>N</sup>X<sup>n</sup></i> (carriers) &nbsp; (c) 0",
   "w": "Daughters get the father’s only X (<i>X<sup>n</sup></i>) and a normal X from the mother → carriers. Sons get the father’s Y and the mother’s <i>X<sup>N</sup></i> → normal.", "r": "15.2"},
  {"t": "num", "q": "A carrier woman (<i>X<sup>N</sup>X<sup>n</sup></i>) marries a man with normal vision (<i>X<sup>N</sup>Y</i>). Find (a) P(a son is colour-blind), (b) P(a daughter is a carrier), (c) P(their next child — sex not yet known — is colour-blind).",
   "a": "(a) 1/2 &nbsp; (b) 1/2 &nbsp; (c) 1/4",
   "w": "Children: <i>X<sup>N</sup>X<sup>N</sup></i>, <i>X<sup>N</sup>X<sup>n</sup></i>, <i>X<sup>N</sup>Y</i>, <i>X<sup>n</sup>Y</i>, each 1/4. Only <i>X<sup>n</sup>Y</i> is affected → 1/4 of all children, 1/2 of sons.", "r": "15.2"},
  {"t": "num", "q": "A carrier woman (<i>X<sup>N</sup>X<sup>n</sup></i>) marries a colour-blind man (<i>X<sup>n</sup>Y</i>). (a) What is the chance that any one child is colour-blind? (b) What are the normal-vision daughters? (c) What are the normal-vision sons?",
   "a": "(a) 1/2, for sons and daughters alike &nbsp; (b) carriers (<i>X<sup>N</sup>X<sup>n</sup></i>) &nbsp; (c) <i>X<sup>N</sup>Y</i> — they carry no recessive allele",
   "w": "Children: <i>X<sup>N</sup>X<sup>n</sup></i>, <i>X<sup>n</sup>X<sup>n</sup></i>, <i>X<sup>N</sup>Y</i>, <i>X<sup>n</sup>Y</i>, each 1/4. Affected: <i>X<sup>n</sup>X<sup>n</sup></i> + <i>X<sup>n</sup>Y</i> = 1/2.", "r": "15.2"},
  {"t": "num", "q": "Morgan’s reciprocal cross: a white-eyed female (<i>X<sup>w</sup>X<sup>w</sup></i>) is mated with a red-eyed male (<i>X<sup>w+</sup>Y</i>). Predict the F<sub>1</sub> phenotypes of each sex.",
   "a": "All F<sub>1</sub> females red-eyed (<i>X<sup>w+</sup>X<sup>w</sup></i>); all F<sub>1</sub> males white-eyed (<i>X<sup>w</sup>Y</i>)",
   "w": "Sons receive their only X from the mother (<i>X<sup>w</sup></i>), so they are white. Daughters receive <i>X<sup>w+</sup></i> from the father, which is dominant.", "r": "15.1–15.2"},
  {"t": "num", "q": "In Morgan’s original F<sub>2</sub> (from F<sub>1</sub> red female × F<sub>1</sub> red male), out of 400 flies, how many white-eyed flies are expected, and how many of them are female?",
   "a": "100 white-eyed; 0 female", "w": "F<sub>1</sub>: <i>X<sup>w+</sup>X<sup>w</sup></i> × <i>X<sup>w+</sup>Y</i>. F<sub>2</sub>: females all red; males ½ red, ½ white. White = 1/4 of all = 100, all male.", "r": "15.1"},
  {"t": "ar", "A": "X-linked recessive disorders are much more common in males than in females.",
   "R": "Males are hemizygous, so a single recessive allele on their X chromosome is expressed.", "a": 0,
   "e": "A female needs two copies (homozygous recessive) to be affected.", "r": "15.2"},
  {"t": "ar", "A": "Polyploid organisms are usually more normal in appearance than aneuploid ones.",
   "R": "Polyploidy is common in plants.", "a": 1,
   "e": "Both are true, but R does not explain A. The explanation (Campbell 15.4): one extra or missing chromosome upsets genetic balance more than an entire extra set does.", "r": "15.4"},
  {"t": "tf", "q": "Polyploidy is common in animals but rare in plants.", "a": False, "e": "False — it is common in plants and rare in animals.", "r": "15.4"},
  {"t": "tf", "q": "The frequency of Down syndrome increases with the age of the mother.", "a": True, "e": "True.", "r": "15.4"},
  {"t": "fill", "q": "In mammals, females are ____ and males are ____. Every ovum carries an ____ chromosome, while ____ % of sperm carry a Y.",
   "a": ["XX", "XY", "X", "50"], "e": "So the father’s sperm decides the sex of the child.", "r": "15.2"},
  {"t": "short", "q": "Explain why a colour-blind father can never pass colour blindness to his son, yet every one of his daughters is a carrier.",
   "a": "The gene is on the X chromosome. A father gives his X to every daughter and his Y to every son. So all daughters receive his <i>X<sup>n</sup></i> (and, with a normal mother, become carriers), while sons receive his Y and get their X — and their colour-vision allele — from the mother.", "r": "15.2"},
 ]},
