{"id": "E", "title": "Cell communication and signal transduction", "lec": "Lecture 20", "camp": "Campbell Ch. 11",
 "qs": [
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "When food is scarce, cells of the soil bacterium <i>Myxococcus xanthus</i>:",
   "o": ["divide faster", "send out a chemical signal, aggregate and form fruiting bodies containing spores",
         "fuse to form a diploid cell", "switch mating type"], "a": 1, "e": "Shows that signalling is not unique to multicellular organisms.", "r": "11.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Quorum sensing allows bacteria to:",
   "o": ["sense the local population density through the concentration of signalling molecules", "detect light",
         "exchange DNA by conjugation", "move towards food"], "a": 0,
   "e": "‘Quorum’ = the smallest number of members needed before a group can make a decision.", "r": "11.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "In baker’s yeast, the ‘a factor’ secreted by type-a cells binds to receptors on:",
   "o": ["other type-a cells", "type-α cells", "the a cell’s own nucleus", "bacterial cells"], "a": 1,
   "e": "Each mating factor binds to its specific receptor on the opposite mating type, triggering responses that lead to cell fusion.", "r": "11.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Fusion of an a cell and an α cell produces:",
   "o": ["two haploid a cells", "an a/α diploid cell containing all the genes of both cells", "a spore-forming fruiting body", "a gamete"],
   "a": 1, "e": "The a/α diploid can divide by mitosis or undergo meiosis to give haploids.", "r": "11.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Junctions that directly connect the cytoplasm of adjacent cells are called ____ in animals and ____ in plants.",
   "o": ["synapses; cell walls", "gap junctions; plasmodesmata", "plasmodesmata; gap junctions", "tight junctions; stomata"], "a": 1,
   "e": "Signalling substances pass freely between neighbouring cells through them.", "r": "11.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "A growth factor released by one cell that stimulates nearby target cells to grow and divide is an example of:",
   "o": ["endocrine signalling", "paracrine signalling", "synaptic signalling", "cell–cell recognition"], "a": 1,
   "e": "Para- = beside. Local regulators travel only short distances.", "r": "11.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Hormones that travel through the bloodstream and act only on cells carrying the right receptors illustrate:",
   "o": ["long-distance (endocrine) signalling", "paracrine signalling", "gap-junction signalling", "quorum sensing"], "a": 0,
   "e": "The hormone reaches every cell, but only cells with its receptor respond.", "r": "11.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "The correct order of the three stages of cell signalling is:",
   "o": ["transduction → reception → response", "reception → response → transduction", "reception → transduction → response", "response → reception → transduction"],
   "a": 2, "e": "Analogy from the slide: knock at the door (receptor, the ‘discriminator’) → message carried inside (the ‘transducer’) → action triggered (the ‘amplifier’).", "r": "11.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "A G protein is in its active (ON) state when it is bound to:",
   "o": ["ATP", "GDP", "GTP", "cAMP"], "a": 2, "e": "GDP-bound = inactive; GTP-bound = active.", "r": "11.2"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "A G protein switches itself off by:",
   "o": ["leaving the plasma membrane", "hydrolysing its bound GTP to GDP and P<sub>i</sub> (GTPase activity)",
         "binding a second signalling molecule", "being degraded in the lysosome"], "a": 1,
   "e": "This built-in control makes the response transient once the signal is gone.", "r": "11.2"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "G protein-coupled receptors share a common architecture of:",
   "o": ["two subunits that dimerize", "seven transmembrane domains", "a single ion channel pore", "an intracellular DNA-binding domain"],
   "a": 1, "e": "GPCRs are extremely widespread (vision, smell, taste, development…).", "r": "11.2"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Adenylyl cyclase converts:",
   "o": ["cAMP → AMP", "ATP → cAMP", "GTP → GDP", "glycogen → glucose 1-phosphate"], "a": 1,
   "e": "Phosphodiesterase converts cAMP → AMP, switching the second messenger off.", "r": "11.3"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Which pair received the 1994 Nobel Prize for discovering G proteins?",
   "o": ["Watson and Crick", "Alfred Gilman and Martin Rodbell", "Earl Sutherland and Thomas Hunt Morgan", "Avery and McCarty"], "a": 1,
   "e": "Earl W. Sutherland won the 1971 Nobel Prize (hormone action, cAMP as second messenger).", "r": "11.1, 11.3"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Approximately what fraction of all medicines in use today act on GPCRs?",
   "keep": True, "o": ["~6%", "~20%", "~60%", "~95%"], "a": 2, "e": "Example classes on the slide: β-blockers (metoprolol, atenolol), albuterol for asthma, antipsychotics acting on D<sub>2</sub> dopamine receptors.", "r": "11.2"},
  {"t": "multi", "q": "Which of the following can be a cellular <b>response</b> at the end of a signalling pathway?",
   "o": ["Catalysis of a reaction by an activated enzyme", "Activation of specific genes (gene expression)",
         "Rearrangement of the cytoskeleton", "A change in the cell’s DNA sequence"], "a": [0, 1, 2],
   "e": "The three response types listed on the slide. Signalling does not rewrite the DNA sequence.", "r": "11.4"},
  {"t": "match", "q": "Match the mode of signalling with its description.",
   "L": ["Gap junctions", "Plasmodesmata", "Paracrine", "Synaptic", "Endocrine"],
   "R": ["Hormone carried by blood to distant targets", "Channels joining the cytoplasm of adjacent plant cells",
         "Local regulator (e.g. growth factor) acts on nearby cells", "Neurotransmitter diffuses across a synapse",
         "Channels joining the cytoplasm of adjacent animal cells"], "a": [4, 1, 2, 3, 0], "e": "Local: junctions, cell–cell recognition, paracrine, synaptic. Long-distance: hormones.", "r": "11.1"},
  {"t": "match", "q": "Match the molecule with its role in the adrenaline (epinephrine) pathway.",
   "L": ["Adrenaline", "cAMP", "Adenylyl cyclase", "Phosphodiesterase", "Protein kinase A"],
   "R": ["Second messenger", "Converts cAMP → AMP", "First messenger (hormone)", "Activated by cAMP; phosphorylates other proteins", "Converts ATP → cAMP"],
   "a": [2, 0, 4, 1, 3], "e": "First messenger outside the cell → second messenger inside.", "r": "11.3"},
  {"t": "order", "q": "Arrange the events of GPCR signalling in order.",
   "items": ["The activated receptor binds an inactive G protein", "A signalling molecule (ligand) binds the GPCR on the outside",
             "GTPase activity hydrolyses GTP; the G protein becomes inactive again", "The receptor changes shape (conformation)",
             "The G protein swaps GDP for GTP, leaves the receptor and activates an enzyme"],
   "a": [1, 3, 0, 4, 2], "e": "Ligand → shape change → G protein binds → GDP/GTP exchange & enzyme activation → GTP hydrolysis (off).", "r": "11.2"},
  {"t": "num", "q": "In the adrenaline cascade on the slide, 1 adrenaline molecule → 10<sup>2</sup> active G proteins → 10<sup>2</sup> adenylyl cyclase → 10<sup>4</sup> cAMP → 10<sup>4</sup> PKA → 10<sup>5</sup> phosphorylase kinase → 10<sup>6</sup> glycogen phosphorylase → 10<sup>8</sup> glucose 1-phosphate. (a) What is the overall amplification? (b) Name the three steps that each amplify the signal 100-fold. (c) How many glucose 1-phosphate molecules result per active PKA molecule?",
   "a": "(a) 10<sup>8</sup>-fold &nbsp; (b) the receptor activating G proteins (1 → 10<sup>2</sup>), adenylyl cyclase making cAMP (10<sup>2</sup> → 10<sup>4</sup>), and glycogen phosphorylase releasing glucose 1-phosphate (10<sup>6</sup> → 10<sup>8</sup>) &nbsp; (c) 10<sup>4</sup>",
   "w": "(a) 10<sup>8</sup>/1. (b) Step-to-step ratios: 100, 1, 100, 1, 10, 10, 100 — three steps of 100×. (c) 10<sup>8</sup>/10<sup>4</sup> = 10<sup>4</sup>.", "r": "11.4"},
  {"t": "ar", "A": "In cholera, intestinal cells keep producing cAMP and secrete large amounts of salt and water.",
   "R": "Cholera toxin modifies a G protein so that it cannot hydrolyse GTP and stays active.", "a": 0,
   "e": "The stuck-ON G protein keeps stimulating adenylyl cyclase. (Campbell 11.3.)", "r": "11.3"},
  {"t": "ar", "A": "One adrenaline molecule bound to a liver cell can release only a few molecules of glucose 1-phosphate.",
   "R": "In a signalling cascade, each activated molecule activates many molecules of the next step.", "a": 3, "e": "A is false — the cascade releases about 10<sup>8</sup> molecules. R is true, and it is exactly why A is false (amplification).", "r": "11.3–11.4"},
  {"t": "tf", "q": "Signalling between cells is found only in multicellular organisms.", "a": False,
   "e": "False — bacteria (<i>Myxococcus</i>, quorum sensing) and yeast use it too. Signalling mechanisms are evolutionarily conserved.", "r": "11.1"},
  {"t": "fill", "q": "To ‘transduce’ means to ____. In the doorbell analogy, the receptor acts as the ____, the relay molecules as the ____, and the cascade as the ____.",
   "a": ["convert energy or a message into another form", "discriminator", "transducer", "amplifier"], "e": "From the slide ‘a knock on the door’.", "r": "11.1"},
  {"t": "short", "q": "“What is the advantage of a cascade?” (slide A → B → E → F). Give three advantages.",
   "a": "(1) Amplification — each active molecule activates many of the next, so one hormone molecule can release ~10<sup>8</sup> product molecules. (2) Regulation — every step is a point where the signal can be tuned, blocked or switched off. (3) Coordination/branching — one signal can spread to several responses, and inputs from different pathways can be combined.", "r": "11.3–11.4"},
  {"t": "short", "q": "A drug blocks phosphodiesterase in liver cells responding to adrenaline. Predict the effect, with reasoning.",
   "a": "cAMP is no longer converted to AMP, so cAMP stays high after the adrenaline is gone. Protein kinase A remains active, the cascade keeps running and glycogen breakdown (glucose 1-phosphate release) is prolonged — the response is stronger and lasts longer.", "r": "11.3"},
 ]},
