{"id": "F", "title": "Big data in biology and bioinformatics", "lec": "Lecture 21", "camp": "Lecture slides; Campbell 16.1, 21.2",
 "qs": [
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "The suffixes ‘-ome’ and ‘-omics’ mean, respectively:",
   "o": ["the study of; the complete set", "the complete set; the study of an -ome", "a single gene; many genes", "protein; RNA"],
   "a": 1, "e": "Genome = all the DNA; genomics = the study of genomes.", "r": "21.2"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "The proteome is:",
   "o": ["the complete set of proteins of a cell or organism", "the complete set of RNA transcripts", "the set of all metabolites", "the community of microbes in the gut"],
   "a": 0, "e": "Transcriptome = RNA transcripts; metabolome = metabolites; microbiome = microbial community.", "r": "21.2"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Computer-aided analysis of protein data — the beginning of bioinformatics, around 1960 — was pioneered by:",
   "o": ["Watson, Crick and Franklin", "Margaret Dayhoff, Richard Eck and Robert Ledley", "Hassabis and Jumper", "Gilman and Rodbell"],
   "a": 1, "e": "Their work led to the Protein Information Resource (PIR) database.", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "The metric prefix ‘exa-’ stands for:", "keep": True, "o": ["10<sup>12</sup>", "10<sup>15</sup>", "10<sup>18</sup>", "10<sup>21</sup>"],
   "a": 2, "e": "kilo 10<sup>3</sup>, mega 10<sup>6</sup>, giga 10<sup>9</sup>, tera 10<sup>12</sup>, peta 10<sup>15</sup>, exa 10<sup>18</sup>, zetta 10<sup>21</sup>.", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "For B-DNA (the common form), the table on the slide gives:",
   "o": ["11 bp per turn, 2.55 Å between base pairs", "10 bp per turn, 3.4 Å between base pairs, diameter 20 Å",
         "12 bp per turn, left-handed", "10 bp per turn, diameter 23 Å"], "a": 1,
   "e": "So one turn = 34 Å = 3.4 nm and the helix is 2 nm wide (Campbell 16.1).", "r": "16.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Z-DNA differs from B-DNA because it is:",
   "o": ["left-handed, with about 12 bp per turn", "right-handed, with 11 bp per turn", "single-stranded", "made of RNA"],
   "a": 0, "e": "Its rotation per base pair is negative (−30°), i.e. left-handed. A-DNA: right-handed, 11 bp/turn, wider (23 Å).", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Methods that derive a <i>post hoc</i> understanding of what a trained model has learnt are called:",
   "o": ["interpretable models", "explainable methods", "black boxes", "databases"], "a": 1,
   "e": "Interpretable models are intelligible by design (their parameters and architecture can be understood directly).", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Which of these is <b>NOT</b> a reason for comparing biological sequences?",
   "o": ["To infer the function of a sequence from a better-characterised one", "To find whether genes or proteins are evolutionarily related",
         "To find similar regions such as catalytic or binding sites", "To weigh a protein directly"], "a": 3,
   "e": "The other three reasons are on the ‘Why compare biological sequences?’ slide.", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "‘Resequencing’ an individual’s genome means:",
   "o": ["sequencing a new species for the first time", "mapping the sequencing reads to a known reference genome to find differences such as SNPs and indels",
         "translating DNA into protein", "repeating Sanger sequencing on the same sample"], "a": 1, "e": "Indel = insertion or deletion; SNP = single-nucleotide polymorphism.", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "The 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry was awarded to:",
   "o": ["Gilman and Rodbell for G proteins", "David Baker, Demis Hassabis and John Jumper for computational protein design and structure prediction (AlphaFold)",
         "Watson and Crick for the double helix", "Dayhoff for the PIR database"], "a": 1, "e": "AlphaFold predicts 3D protein structures at near-atomic accuracy.", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "From the ‘methods for extracting structural information’ chart, which technique <b>cannot</b> resolve individual atoms?",
   "o": ["X-ray crystallography", "NMR", "Electron microscopy", "Light microscopy"], "a": 3,
   "e": "Light microscopy works from roughly cells/bacteria upward; X-ray crystallography, NMR and EM reach ~1 Å.", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "multi", "q": "According to the AlphaFold slide, which inputs feed the deep neural network that predicts a protein’s 3D structure?",
   "o": ["The amino-acid sequence of the target protein", "Its evolutionary history (related sequences)",
         "Known 3D structures of proteins (whatever is available)", "The mass spectrum of the protein"], "a": [0, 1, 2],
   "e": "Output: atomic-level 3D structure, with very high, independently verified accuracy.", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "match", "q": "Match the ‘-ome’ with what it is the complete set of.",
   "L": ["Genome", "Transcriptome", "Proteome", "Metabolome", "Microbiome"],
   "R": ["All the proteins", "All the small-molecule metabolites", "All the DNA (hereditary information)", "The microbial community (and its genes)", "All the RNA transcripts"],
   "a": [2, 4, 0, 1, 3], "e": "Other -omes on the slide: epigenome, lipidome, glycome.", "r": "21.2"},
  {"t": "match", "q": "Match the metric prefix with its power of ten.",
   "L": ["kilo-", "giga-", "peta-", "exa-", "zetta-"], "R": ["10<sup>21</sup>", "10<sup>9</sup>", "10<sup>3</sup>", "10<sup>18</sup>", "10<sup>15</sup>"],
   "a": [2, 1, 4, 3, 0], "e": "Genomics (2015 estimate): ~1 zetta-base acquired per year; 2–40 exabytes of storage.", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "match", "q": "Bioinformatics sits where three fields meet. Match each field with its role.",
   "L": ["Statistics", "Computer science", "Biology (domain knowledge)"],
   "R": ["Develop algorithms; storage and retrieval of data", "Interpretation, hypothesis testing, context for data generation",
         "Choice of metrics, pattern extraction, validity of inferences"], "a": [2, 0, 1], "e": "From the three-circle slide.", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "num", "q": "Using the B-DNA rise of 3.4 Å per base pair, how long would the DNA of one haploid human genome (3.0 × 10<sup>9</sup> bp) be if stretched out? How much DNA is in one diploid cell?",
   "a": "≈ 1.02 m per haploid genome; ≈ 2 m per diploid cell", "w": "3.0 × 10<sup>9</sup> × 3.4 × 10<sup>−10</sup> m = 1.02 m. Diploid = 2 × 1.02 ≈ 2.04 m — all packed into a nucleus a few µm across.", "r": "16.1"},
  {"t": "num", "q": "A 1000-bp stretch of DNA is in the B form. (a) How many helical turns does it make? (b) What is its length? (c) How many turns would it make in the A form?",
   "a": "(a) 100 turns &nbsp; (b) 340 nm (3400 Å) &nbsp; (c) ≈ 91 turns", "w": "(a) 1000 ÷ 10. (b) 1000 × 3.4 Å = 3400 Å = 340 nm. (c) 1000 ÷ 11 = 90.9.", "r": "16.1"},
  {"t": "num", "q": "Each DNA base (A, C, G or T) can be stored in 2 bits. How much storage (in megabytes) does one 3.0 × 10<sup>9</sup>-base human genome need? (1 byte = 8 bits.)",
   "a": "750 MB", "w": "3.0 × 10<sup>9</sup> × 2 bits = 6.0 × 10<sup>9</sup> bits = 7.5 × 10<sup>8</sup> bytes = 750 MB. Raw reads (with quality scores and repeated coverage) need far more, which is why genomics is ‘big data’.", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "tf", "q": "‘-Omics’ studies make one-protein-at-a-time studies unnecessary.", "a": False,
   "e": "False — they are complementary: they give different types of information and answer different questions.", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "tf", "q": "Data generation in biology has become affordable, which is one reason biology produces very large data sets.", "a": True, "e": "True (with high-throughput -omics and advances in chemistry, optics, automation and computing).", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "fill", "q": "SNP stands for ____; an indel is an ____ or ____; ChIP-Seq maps the binding sites of ____ on the genome.",
   "a": ["single-nucleotide polymorphism", "insertion", "deletion", "transcription factors"], "e": "ChIP = chromatin immunoprecipitation.", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "ar", "A": "Genomics generates data on a scale comparable to astronomy and YouTube.",
   "R": "Bioinformatics began around 1960 with computer-aided analysis of protein sequences.", "a": 1, "e": "Both true (PLoS Biol 2015 comparison; Dayhoff, Eck and Ledley), but R is history — the reason for today’s data scale is affordable high-throughput -omics.", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "short", "q": "Give the two definitions of bioinformatics by Higgs and Attwood.",
   "a": "(1) The development of computational methods for studying the structure, function and evolution of genes, proteins and whole genomes. (2) The development of methods for the management and analysis of biological information arising from genomics and high-throughput experiments. (Broad slide definition: analyse biological data to extract patterns and generate hypotheses.)", "r": "—"},
  {"t": "short", "q": "Why is the ‘black box’ nature of AI/ML a concern in biology, and what are the two approaches to it?",
   "a": "A black-box model may predict well, but it does not tell us <i>why</i> it made a prediction or what biological pattern underlies it — and biology needs mechanisms and testable hypotheses. Explainable methods analyse a trained model afterwards (post hoc) to reveal what it learnt; interpretable models are designed so that their parameters and architecture are understandable from the start.", "r": "—"},
 ]},
