{"id": "G", "title": "Evolution and natural selection", "lec": "Lecture 22", "camp": "Campbell Ch. 22, 23 (opening), 24.1",
 "qs": [
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Darwin’s <i>On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection</i> was published in:",
   "keep": True, "o": ["1809", "1859", "1866", "1953"], "a": 1, "e": "1809 = Darwin’s birth year; 1866 = Mendel’s paper.", "r": "22.2"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Darwin’s three broad observations about nature were:",
   "o": ["mutation, drift and gene flow", "the unity of life, the diversity of life, and the match between organisms and their environments",
         "segregation, independent assortment and dominance", "reception, transduction and response"], "a": 1, "e": "He explained all three with descent with modification by natural selection.", "r": "22 intro"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "According to the biological species concept, a species is a group of populations whose members:",
   "o": ["look alike", "can interbreed in nature and produce viable, fertile offspring", "live in the same place", "have identical DNA"],
   "a": 1, "e": "…but cannot do so with members of other such groups.", "r": "24.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "A male donkey × female horse gives a mule; a female donkey × male horse gives a hinny. Both are sterile. This shows that:",
   "o": ["horses and donkeys are the same species", "horses and donkeys are separate species", "hybrids are always fertile", "donkeys evolved from horses recently"],
   "a": 1, "e": "Offspring are viable but not fertile, so the parents belong to different species.", "r": "24.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "The biological species concept cannot be applied to:",
   "o": ["birds", "mammals", "organisms that reproduce only asexually (divide to multiply)", "flowering plants"], "a": 2,
   "e": "Interbreeding is the test, so asexual organisms (and fossils) are excluded.", "r": "24.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "An adaptation is:",
   "o": ["any change an individual makes during its lifetime", "an inherited characteristic that enhances survival and reproduction in a specific environment",
         "a random mutation", "a learned behaviour"], "a": 1, "e": "Example: finch beak shapes suited to their food.", "r": "22.2"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Malthus influenced Darwin with the idea that:",
   "o": ["acquired characteristics are inherited", "populations increase exponentially while food supply increases only linearly",
         "species are fixed", "the Earth is only a few thousand years old"], "a": 1, "e": "So more offspring are produced than can survive — a struggle for existence.", "r": "22.2"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "After the 1977 drought on Daphne Major, the average beak depth of the medium ground finch population increased because:",
   "o": ["each bird’s beak grew larger to crack hard seeds", "birds with deeper beaks could crack the plentiful large, hard seeds, survived and reproduced",
         "the drought caused mutations for larger beaks", "small-beaked birds migrated away"], "a": 1,
   "e": "Small soft seeds were scarce; about 180 of ~1200 birds survived. The population evolved — no individual beak changed.", "r": "23 intro"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Which statement is correct?",
   "o": ["Individuals evolve during their lifetime", "Natural selection acts on individuals, but it is populations that evolve over time",
         "Natural selection is goal-directed", "Natural selection creates the variation it acts on"], "a": 1, "e": "Key point 1 on the ‘three key points’ slide.", "r": "22.2"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "According to the lecture, the enzyme that replicates DNA makes roughly:",
   "o": ["no mistakes", "1 error in every 10<sup>4</sup>–10<sup>5</sup> nucleotides added", "1 error in every 10 nucleotides", "1 error per genome"],
   "a": 1, "e": "Many errors are repaired, but some remain — a basis of variability.", "r": "23.1"},
  {"t": "mcq", "q": "Methicillin kills <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> by:",
   "o": ["blocking DNA replication", "inhibiting a protein that the bacteria use to build their cell walls", "dissolving the cell membrane", "blocking ribosomes"],
   "a": 1, "e": "MRSA strains make a different protein, so they survive methicillin and out-reproduce non-resistant strains.", "r": "22.3"},
  {"t": "match", "q": "Match the Galápagos finch with its beak and diet.",
   "L": ["Large ground finch (<i>Geospiza magnirostris</i>)", "Cactus ground finch (<i>Geospiza scandens</i>)", "Green warbler finch (<i>Certhidea olivacea</i>)"],
   "R": ["Narrow, pointed beak — grasping insects", "Large but short beak — cracking seeds that fall from trees", "Long, sharp beak — tearing cactus flowers and pulp"],
   "a": [1, 2, 0], "e": "Beak shape is matched to food — an adaptation.", "r": "22.2"},
  {"t": "match", "q": "Match each of Darwin’s four components of natural selection with its description.",
   "L": ["Variation", "Inheritance", "High rate of population growth", "Differential survival and reproduction"],
   "R": ["Individuals with traits suited to the struggle for local resources leave more offspring", "More offspring each year than local resources can support",
         "Individuals differ in size, colour, markings, voice…", "Some traits are passed consistently from parent to offspring"],
   "a": [2, 3, 1, 0], "e": "Slides ‘1 of 4’ to ‘4 of 4’.", "r": "22.2"},
  {"t": "ar", "A": "After the drought, the proportion of large-beaked finches in the population increased.",
   "R": "Individual finches grew larger beaks during the drought.", "a": 2,
   "e": "A is true; R is false — each bird’s beak stayed the same size; the population changed.", "r": "23 intro"},
  {"t": "ar", "A": "A trait favoured in one place or time may be useless or harmful in another.",
   "R": "Environmental factors vary from place to place and over time.", "a": 0, "e": "Key point 3 on the slide.", "r": "22.2"},
  {"t": "tf", "q": "Natural selection can produce any new trait that a population needs.", "a": False,
   "e": "False. It can only amplify or diminish heritable traits that already differ among individuals.", "r": "22.2"},
  {"t": "tf", "q": "<i>S. aureus</i> became resistant to penicillin in 1945, two years after the drug was first widely used.", "a": True,
   "e": "True. Methicillin resistance followed in 1961, again two years after wide use.", "r": "22.3"},
  {"t": "fill", "q": "Darwin sailed on HMS ____; the islands whose finches and tortoises influenced him most are the ____ Islands.",
   "a": ["Beagle", "Galápagos"], "e": "He collected specimens especially from islands, i.e. isolated populations.", "r": "22.2"},
  {"t": "short", "q": "Classify each as <b>natural</b> or <b>artificial</b> selection: (i) nature ‘decides’; (ii) selective breeding; (iii) breeding Dalmatians; (iv) finch beaks after a drought; (v) inbreeding occurs.",
   "a": "(i) natural; (ii) artificial; (iii) artificial; (iv) natural; (v) artificial. Darwin’s argument: if humans can cause big changes in a short time, nature can do so over a long time.", "r": "22.2"},
  {"t": "short", "q": "Explain, step by step, how MRSA evolved by natural selection.",
   "a": "<i>S. aureus</i> populations contain variation, including rare cells with a different cell-wall protein that methicillin cannot inhibit. When methicillin is used, susceptible cells die while resistant cells survive and reproduce, passing on the resistance gene. Over generations the resistant proportion rises, giving MRSA. The drug did not create the mutation; it selected for it.", "r": "22.3"},
  {"t": "short", "q": "State Darwin’s two observations and two inferences (Campbell Concept 22.2).",
   "a": "Observation 1: members of a population vary in their inherited traits. Observation 2: all species can produce more offspring than the environment can support, so many fail to survive and reproduce. Inference 1: individuals whose inherited traits give a higher probability of surviving and reproducing leave more offspring. Inference 2: this unequal survival and reproduction leads to accumulation of favourable traits in the population over generations.", "r": "22.2"},
 ]},
